Research and References
Breath capture (why aerosol and condensate work)
THC in Breath Aerosols Collected with an Impaction Filter Device Before and After Cannabis Inhalation (pilot study), NCBI/PMC
https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10201171/
Supports: THC travels in breath as aerosol, not vapour, which is why we condense the breath.


Cannabinoids Detected in Exhaled Breath Condensate After Cannabis Use, NCBI/PMC
https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11264354/
Supports: condensing breath into liquid recovers THC and other cannabinoids.


Detection of Δ9-Tetrahydrocannabinol in Exhaled Breath After Cannabis Smoking and Comparison with Oral Fluid, Forensic Toxicology (Springer, 2016)
https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11419-016-0333-x
Supports: THC is measurable in breath after smoking.

Electrochemical detection (DPV on carbon electrodes)
Recent Advances in Electrochemical Sensor Technologies for THC Detection: A Narrative Review, Journal of Cannabis Research (2022)
https://jcannabisresearch.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s42238-022-00122-3
Supports: electrochemical sensing of THC.


Ott, C.E. et al. Electrochemical Detection of Fentanyl with Screen-Printed Carbon Electrodes Using Square-Wave Adsorptive Stripping Voltammetry for Forensic Applications, Journal of Electroanalytical Chemistry, 873, 114425 (2020)
https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jelechem.2020.114425
Supports: fentanyl peaks near 0.75 V and 0.88 V on SPCE, plus interference testing.
Jordan, P.H. and Hart, J.P. Voltammetric Behaviour of Morphine at a Glassy Carbon Electrode, Analyst, 116, 991–996 (1991)
https://pubs.rsc.org/en/content/articlelanding/1991/an/an9911600991
Supports: the morphine oxidation mechanism behind our two-peak opioid signature.


Open-source hardware (potentiostat)
Karlovits, I. et al. Comparison of Cyclic Voltammetry Measurements of Paper-Based Screen-Printed Electrodes via Proprietary and Open-Source Potentiostats, BioResources, 16(2), 3916–3933 (2021)
https://bioresources.cnr.ncsu.edu/wp-content/uploads/2021/04/BioRes_16_2_3916_Karlovits_KTR_Cyclic_Voltammetry_Paper_Printed_Electrode_Potentiostat_18442.pdf
Supports: open-source potentiostat with screen-printed electrodes is scientifically valid.


Rodeostat Open-Source Potentiostat, IO Rodeo (OSHWA certified, UID US002129)
https://certification.oshwa.org/us002129.html
Supports: open hardware documentation, and the Python library we run on the Raspberry Pi.

VOC array (corroborating channel)
Setiawan, D. and Lelono, D. Design of Electronic Nose to Discriminate Mouth Smell of Smokers and Non-Smokers, Universitas Gadjah Mada
https://etd.repository.ugm.ac.id/penelitian/detail/150890
Supports: a metal-oxide sensor array with PCA separated smokers from non-smokers at 92.5% accuracy. This is someone else's result, not ours.
